<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android's backup takes, on API 26 to 30.

  `data_extraction_rules.xml` is the same list for Android 12 and above, which is
  what the owner's phone reads; this file is what an older one would. The two are
  asserted identical by `BackupRulesTest`, because a file only an untested phone
  reads is exactly the copy that drifts unnoticed.

  It is an ALLOW-LIST: naming any `include` means nothing else is taken. That is the
  point. This app holds twelve years of one person's bank messages and the rule it
  lived by until 2026-09-10 was that none of it ever left the device. Backup is now
  on because a new phone otherwise starts empty - the 185 categories he filed by
  hand and the 35 merchant rules he taught the app exist in no bank message and
  cannot be recaptured - so what leaves is written down rather than left to a
  default that would sweep up whatever the app stores next.

  The write-ahead log is listed with the database because Room runs in WAL mode:
  everything committed since the last checkpoint lives in that file alone, and a
  database restored without it quietly loses the newest transactions.
  `MasroufBackupAgent` checkpoints before the copy, so in practice the log is empty
  and small - it is listed for the case where the checkpoint could not run.

  The shared-memory file is deliberately absent. SQLite rebuilds it from the two
  above, and a stale copy restored beside them is a liability, not a saving.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="database" path="masrouf.db"/>
    <include domain="database" path="masrouf.db-wal"/>
    <include domain="sharedpref" path="masrouf.settings.xml"/>
</full-backup-content>
